var e=`<p>Fees in this sector are usually quoted as one number that is not the total, which is exactly why people get caught out later. Here is how the cost of a degree is actually built up, so you can compare any two offers on the same terms.</p>
<h2>What makes up the real total</h2>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Tuition</strong>, per semester or per year, multiplied by the number of semesters. This is the largest component.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Registration or enrolment</strong>, usually charged once at admission.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Examination fees</strong>, per semester, and the charge to re-sit a paper.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Certificates</strong>: the degree certificate, consolidated marksheet and provisional certificate, and a migration certificate where you need one.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Attestation</strong>, if the degree will be used abroad.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The figure in an advertisement is nearly always the first line only. Two programmes with identical tuition can differ substantially once examination and certification charges are added, and the cheaper tuition is sometimes the more expensive degree. Ask for all five lines in writing before you compare anything.</p>
<h2>How the instalments work</h2>
<p>Most universities already charge by semester, which is a form of instalment: you pay as you progress rather than up front. For many people that is enough.</p>
<p>Where a semester payment is still too large in one piece, our plans divide the fee into monthly payments. There is no interest and no processing fee, so the total you pay across the months equals the fee paid up front. The first instalment is due at enrolment and the rest fall on the same date each month. The schedule is agreed in writing at the start, not negotiated later.</p>
<p>When you compare any instalment arrangement, ours or anyone else&#39;s, check three things: the total you will have paid at the end, whether that is higher than paying in full, and what happens if you miss a payment.</p>
<h2>Questions worth asking any provider</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>Is the fee fixed for the full duration, or revised each year?</p>
</li>
<li><p>What happens if I take a break in study for a semester?</p>
</li>
<li><p>What is refundable, and by when, if I withdraw early?</p>
</li>
<li><p>Is the fee paid to the university directly, and will I get the university&#39;s receipt?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>That last one is a safety question more than a financial one. A fee paid to a study centre in its own name, with no receipt from the university, is the arrangement behind most of the losses in this field.</p>
<h2>What we will not do</h2>
<p>We do not take payment before you have seen the university name, the programme, its <a href="https://deb.ugc.ac.in/">DEB</a> entitlement and the full fee in writing. If any provider asks you to pay to &quot;block a seat&quot; before showing you those, treat it as a warning rather than urgency.</p>
<h2>Keep the paperwork</h2>
<p>Keep every receipt with your enrolment number. Fee records are asked for at unexpected moments, including at graduation and sometimes years later during verification.</p>
<p>Ask us for a written breakdown for any <a href="/courses">programme on this site</a>. You will get every component, the instalment schedule and what happens if you need to pause, before you commit to anything. If you have a quote from elsewhere and want to know what it leaves out, send it to us.</p>
`;export{e as html};